'''trimestrial''' (trī-mes′tri-a̤l), ''a.'' [< L. ''trimestris'' (see ''trimester'') + ''-al.''] Of or pertaining to a trimester; occurring every three months; quarterly. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''trimetallic''' (trī-me-tal′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. τρεῖς (τρι-) three, + μέταλλον, metal: see ''metallic.''] Pertaining to or involving the use of three metals, as in currency. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The metal coinage system of the world is not therefore mono-metallic, nor bi-metallic, but ''tri-metallic.''
{{right|''Contemporary Rev.'', LII. 812.}}}}

'''trimeter''' (trim′e-tėr), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [= F. ''trimètre'' = It. ''trimetro'', < L. ''trimetrus'', < Gr. τρίμετρος, containing three measures, < τρεῖς (τρι-), three + μέτρον, measure.] '''I.''' ''a.'' In ''pros.'', consisting of three measures, especially of three iambic measures.

'''II.''' ''n.'' In ''pros.'', a verse or period consisting of three measures. {{smaller|A trochaic, iambic, or anapestic trimeter consists of three dipodies (six feet); a trimeter of other rhythms is a hexapody, or period of six feet. The name is specifically given to the iambic trimeter,}}

